    "q-160949": {
      "dom_id": "step-q160949",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14147177/assets/6b0365b0f6b00859fb3739876f7d9a119db8b7f649e1935c66091285460e7f70.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$",
      "worked_solution": "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=0$ and $u=1,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14147177/assets/40d60714c368f92bd7382005882c627be119f6cb05665441730887b60306cdb7.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
