    "q-161034": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161034",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14147177/assets/b3bef2dbb84cfe3a2881abf8f61761f98ea0d9cf4a07ea582355c57d375f975d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 6,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $3$ and $6$ centered at $O.$",
      "worked_solution": "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ and $3\\le u\\le 6.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})),\\,3\\le u\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14147177/assets/b976e62862ba82dfd89ff28627239dd5b5d1be1144e257223c091f29a5729f89.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=3,$ we obtain the point \n$(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}),\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $3.$\n\n- When $u=6,$ we obtain the point \n$(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $6.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $3$ and $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
